    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm working on a pilot formulation for a low-fat mayonnaise variant, but I'm running into some stability issues with the emulsion in high-acid environments (pH ~3.8). Could you explain the specific molecular role of lecithin in stabilizing the oil-water interface under these acidic conditions, and how the charge on the emulsifier might be affecting the droplet coalescence?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Lecithin, being amphiphilic, adsorbs to the oil-water interface, forming a protective molecular barrier around each oil droplet. Under low pH, the ionization state of the lecithin molecules can change, potentially affecting their ability to maintain a strong steric or electrostatic repulsive layer around the droplet. If the surface charge interactions weaken or become insufficiently repulsive in the acidic environment, the droplets can approach each other closely enough to overcome the interfacial film, leading to droplet coalescence and phase separation.",
      "source": "target",
